as it he was difficult but he was also  he cared about  he cared that the look and feel and and  and quality of the stories would be as  good as the devices that he was trying  to sell so that's the journalism side of  that and we you know write what the  journalism part on that level but I  wonder you know the company's so  interesting to everyone in the world of  business who wants to find some kind of  inspiration for the way that Steve Jobs  did think and have the kind of success  that Apple is having in their businesses  and I wonder if when you look at what  the legend of Steve Jobs is it survives  it survives at Apple what are the other  pieces you think that are replicable you  know it's interesting there are so many  books built around the idea that you can  learn shortcuts to success from Steve  Jobs that it's about simplicity or it's  about valuing design or it's about being  tough on your employees the thing that  struck me most about about Steve's  Renaissance and the comeback at Apple is  that none of that is true there are no  shortcuts the only thing that that that  I can you know sort of advocate for out  of Steve's experience is that is the way  he trusted himself he he he learned from  everywhere he had enormous peripheral  vision he could that's what made it so  possible for him to go into the music  industry and into the movie industry  he saw widely and he brought all of that  together in in in side him and agree and  he was and he was an impresario who  could take all of that  and create something brand-new that was  two three four steps better than  anything that had been created before so  to me that's what's you know that's what  stands out about Steve Jobs is an  awareness a willingness to learn so  let's talk about Pixar a little bit  because Pixar was a business that George  Lucas couldn't figure out and this is a  guy who knows something about telling  stories and making movies and and your  book focuses or at least gives a lot of  attention to that that was so important  and I wonder what like what what did  Steve Jobs really bring to Pixar John  Lasseter gets all the credit for the  brilliant movies and I wonder what Steve  Jobs really brought on an active level  to Pixar well you know Jobs was  incredibly lucky with Pixar it was a  side bet that he made shortly after  getting exiled from Apple he his  original idea for the for the company  was all off he wanted Pixar's technology  to be both the basis of its own computer  and to go along with nexts hardware that  he was developing it next eventually he  became the negotiator and the outside  man for the company and those were  critical critical roles he was also the  finance to the company for a while later  I mean Steve's main contribution I think  to to Pixar was as this negotiator an  outside man he arranged he got them a  deal with Disney that led to Toy Story  he renegotiated that deal so that they  could have they were set up for several  more pictures and they were sharing they  were all co-financed  he he's responsible for taking the  company public and he's responsible for  fun eventually finding it a safe haven  with Disney and the Disney Deal you can  look at it as him sort of putting  Lasseter and cattle under the wing of  another guy who is really good at  outside stuff Bob Iger
 

